<!--
    THIS FILE IS OURS. It carries no upstream copyright header because THERE IS NO UPSTREAM FILE
    FOR IT TO COPY: aero-theme-for-avalonia HAS NO ComboBox. All nine of its branches were queried
    through the GitHub trees API for any ComboBox*.axaml and every one returned zero hits; its
    AVMAP/Controls.axaml mentions the two types only inside a commented-out block whose avares://
    URIs point at an assembly that does not exist here. The composition below is authored, and
    tests/TanMenu.Core.Tests/Themes/AeroAttributionTests.cs lists this file by name in the set under
    Themes/Aero/ that are NOT copies and therefore carry no notice.

    WHAT IT DOES REUSE IS NAMED ITEM BY ITEM in the Numbers section below - the chevron geometry is
    ButtonSpinner's, restated under our own key, and the drop-down button is the two-Border
    AeroButton* arrangement Button_RepeatButton_ToggleButton.axaml uses. Both are vocabulary rather
    than markup: a three-point path and a Border-inside-a-Border with the corner-radius seam, named
    where they are used. Nothing here is a copy of an upstream file, which is what the header form
    of §6 attaches to and why this file has no "Source:" line to give.

    Sibling file: ControlStyles/ComboBoxItem.axaml, which paints the popup's rows. That one is NOT
    in the same position and does not claim to be: it is a re-keyed copy of the vendored
    ControlStyles/ListBoxItem.axaml, so it carries the §6 header and is not in NotVendored.

    ── The contract, decompiled rather than assumed ──────────────────────────────────────────────
    Avalonia 12.1's ComboBox declares exactly two template parts,
    [TemplatePart("PART_Popup", typeof(Popup), IsRequired = true)] and
    [TemplatePart("PART_EditableTextBox", typeof(TextBox), IsRequired = false)], and its
    OnApplyTemplate resolves the first with NameScope.Get<Popup> - which THROWS if it is absent -
    and the second with NameScope.Find<TextBox>, which does not. Its ItemsControl base finds
    PART_ItemsPresenter with NameScope.Find<ItemsPresenter>. ComboBoxItem declares no template part
    at all. (ilspycmd over Avalonia.Controls 12.1.0.)

    PART_EditableTextBox is NOT authored: "IsEditable" appears nowhere in this app's source, so an
    editable path would be markup with no call site. PlaceholderText/PlaceholderForeground are not
    honoured either, for the same reason - no combo in this app sets them, and the font combo's
    "no match" case (an uninstalled configured font) is deliberately blank.

    ── The shape, and the one judgement call in it ───────────────────────────────────────────────
    A white field with a gradient button attached to its right edge. Whether Windows 7 painted a
    NON-EDITABLE drop-down list as a white field plus a gradient button, or as one gradient slab,
    is not stated by any Microsoft page: ctrl-drop's sizing figure lives inside
    images/ctrl-drop-image22.png and cannot be read as text, there is no msstyles here and no Aero
    reference capture. AUTHORED: white field plus gradient button, because that is the composition
    the vendored ControlStyles/ButtonSpinner.axaml and NumericUpDown.axaml already use for the same
    job, and internal consistency with the vocabulary already in the tree is a better argument than
    a memory. The 每列个数 spinner sits four rows below these three combos on the same page, so the
    two shapes are seen together.

    Every brush below is one the vendored layer already defines: the body uses the AeroTextBox*
    family, exactly as ButtonSpinner's Border#BgElement does (which is why this body carries the
    same part name), and the button uses the AeroButton* family, exactly as
    Button_RepeatButton_ToggleButton.axaml's Border#Outer / Border#Inner pair does. The popup's
    surface reuses the AeroListBox* family so that a drop-down list and a real ListBox are the same
    object; its rows are ComboBoxItem.axaml, which is a re-keyed copy of the vendored ListBoxItem.

    ── Numbers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      - Height 23. PUBLISHED, https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/vis-layout:
        "combo box / drop-down list / text box height 23".
      - FontSize AeroFontSizeNormal (12) and CornerRadius AeroControlCornerRadius (3), both
        VENDORED from AVMAP/Metrics.axaml.
      - Padding "3,0". The horizontal 3 is ButtonSpinner's Padding; the vertical is 0 rather than
        ButtonSpinner's 3 because this control's height is FIXED at 23. 23 minus the two 1 DIP
        border rows leaves a 21 DIP content box, and a 12 DIP Segoe UI line measures ~16 DIP, so
        3 + 16 + 3 = 22 would be clipped by one row. VerticalContentAlignment=Center centres the
        line in the 21 instead.
      - The drop-down button is 19 DIP wide: a 7 x 4 chevron plus Margin="6,3", which is the
        7 x 4 PathIcon and Padding="6,3" the vendored AeroButtonSpinnerRepeatButton uses, so the
        combo's button and the spinner's buttons below it are the same width. VENDORED shape,
        AUTHORED reuse.
      - The chevron geometry "M 0 0 L 3.5 4 L 7 0" is ButtonSpinnerDecreaseButtonIcon's, restated
        here under our own key rather than reached for across files, so this dictionary's merge
        order carries no dependency.
      - MaxDropDownHeight is NOT set. Avalonia registers it with a default of 200 (decompiled), the
        two three-row combos never reach it, and AppearanceCard.axaml's FontCombo sets 320 itself.

    ── Why the button is two Borders and not a nested ToggleButton ───────────────────────────────
    THE DESIGN DOCUMENT SAYS ToggleButton (§7 Task 6, "the drop-down button is a ToggleButton whose
    Theme is BasedOn={StaticResource {x:Type ToggleButton}}"). It is drawn inline instead, and the
    reason is an input contract, not taste.

    Avalonia 12.1's ComboBox toggles its own drop-down from OnPointerReleased, and that method
    opens with "if (!e.Handled && ...)" - it will not toggle a handled release. Button (and so
    ToggleButton) marks both the press and the release handled. A nested, hit-testable ToggleButton
    therefore either has to drive IsDropDownOpen itself through a TwoWay IsChecked binding, or it
    silently eats every click on the right-hand fifth of the control. THIS MACHINE HAS NO MOUSE -
    GetForegroundWindow() returns 0 and no pointer event can be synthesised - so neither branch
    could have been verified here, and shipping the one that cannot be tested is what this round
    exists to stop. Avalonia's own Fluent ComboBox reaches the same conclusion from the other end:
    its PathIcon#DropDownGlyph sets IsHitTestVisible="False" and the whole control is the click
    target (decompiled from Avalonia.Themes.Fluent 12.1.0). A Border raises no pointer event of its
    own, so every press below reaches the ComboBox, which is the only thing that opens the popup.

    Drawing it inline also buys the states back. A nested ToggleButton would light up only when the
    pointer is over the button itself; these two Borders are driven by the COMBO's :pointerover,
    :pressed, :dropdownopen and :disabled, so hovering anywhere on the control lights the button -
    which is what Windows 7 did.
-->
<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:conv="using:Avalonia.Controls.Converters"
    xmlns:aeconv="using:TanMenu.Wpf.Themes.Aero.Converters"
    x:ClassModifier="internal">

    <StreamGeometry x:Key="AeroComboBoxDropDownGlyph">M 0 0 L 3.5 4 L 7 0</StreamGeometry>

    <!-- The joinery, all four instances of two converters Avalonia itself ships. The body keeps its
         left corners and drops its RIGHT border edge entirely; the button keeps its right corners
         and its full 1 DIP frame, so the seam between them is one line and not two. That is the
         arrangement ControlStyles/ButtonSpinner.axaml uses for the same seam, with the same two
         converter types. -->
    <conv:CornerRadiusFilterConverter x:Key="AeroComboBoxBodyCornerRadiusFilter" Filter="TopLeft,BottomLeft" />
    <conv:MarginMultiplierConverter x:Key="AeroComboBoxBodyBorderThicknessFilter" Indent="1"
                                    Left="True"
                                    Top="True"
                                    Right="False"
                                    Bottom="True" />
    <conv:CornerRadiusFilterConverter x:Key="AeroComboBoxButtonCornerRadiusFilter" Filter="TopRight,BottomRight" />
    <aeconv:AdjustCornerRadiusConverter x:Key="AeroComboBoxButtonInnerCornerRadiiConverter" Amount="-1" />

    <ControlTheme x:Key="{x:Type ComboBox}" TargetType="ComboBox">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroTextBoxIdleBackground}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroTextBoxIdleBorderBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AeroForeground}" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource AeroControlCornerRadius}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource AeroFontSizeNormal}" />
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="23" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="3,0" />
        <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Left" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
        <Setter Property="Focusable" Value="True" />
        <!-- SUPPRESSES THE DEFAULT FOCUS ADORNER, and it has to be here rather than left out.
             Avalonia 12.1's Control.OnGotFocus builds adornerLayer.DefaultFocusAdorner whenever
             IsSet(FocusAdornerProperty) is false and the navigation method is Tab or Directional
             (decompiled) - so a template that simply omits this Setter gets Fluent's adorner, which
             is the two-Border SystemControlFocusVisualPrimary/Secondary rectangle in
             Avalonia.Themes.Fluent 12.1's AdornerLayer theme: the Windows 11 two-ring form that
             §3.2 key #15 of the design deletes. MEASURED, not reasoned: before this Setter existed,
             posting a synthetic WM_KEYDOWN/WM_KEYUP VK_TAB drew a solid black 2+1 px rectangle over
             this control's Aero frame and its rounded corners in Light, white-on-#252525 in Dark.
             UIA SetFocus() CANNOT SEE THAT - it arrives as NavigationMethod.Unspecified, which the
             line above returns on - so a focus check driven only by SetFocus() proves nothing here.
             Fluent's own ComboBox sets the same {x:Null} (decompiled), and so do the two sibling
             Aero controls that likewise repaint their field on :focus, ControlStyles/TextBox.axaml
             and ControlStyles/NumericUpDown.axaml. The vendored Aero Button keeps the default
             adorner instead, and correctly: it declares no :focus rule at all, so the adorner is
             its only focus signal. -->
        <Setter Property="FocusAdorner" Value="{x:Null}" />
        <Setter Property="ScrollViewer.HorizontalScrollBarVisibility" Value="Disabled" />
        <Setter Property="ScrollViewer.VerticalScrollBarVisibility" Value="Auto" />
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <ControlTemplate>
                <Panel>
                    <DockPanel>
                        <Panel x:Name="DropDownButton" DockPanel.Dock="Right">
                            <Border x:Name="DropDownButtonOuter"
                                    Classes="aeroState2"
                                    BorderBrush="{DynamicResource AeroButtonIdleOuterBorderBrush}"
                                    BorderThickness="1"
                                    CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius, Converter={StaticResource AeroComboBoxButtonCornerRadiusFilter}}">
                                <Border x:Name="DropDownButtonInner"
                                        Classes="aeroState2"
                                        Background="{DynamicResource AeroButtonIdleBackground}"
                                        BorderBrush="{DynamicResource AeroButtonIdleInnerBorderBrush}"
                                        BorderThickness="1"
                                        CornerRadius="{Binding #DropDownButtonOuter.CornerRadius, Converter={StaticResource AeroComboBoxButtonInnerCornerRadiiConverter}, Mode=OneWay}" />
                            </Border>
                            <PathIcon x:Name="DropDownGlyph"
                                      Data="{StaticResource AeroComboBoxDropDownGlyph}"
                                      Width="7"
                                      Height="4"
                                      Margin="6,3"
                                      Foreground="{TemplateBinding Foreground}" />
                        </Panel>

                        <Border x:Name="BgElement"
                                Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                                BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                                BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness, Converter={StaticResource AeroComboBoxBodyBorderThicknessFilter}}"
                                CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius, Converter={StaticResource AeroComboBoxBodyCornerRadiusFilter}}">
                            <ContentPresenter x:Name="SelectionBoxPresenter"
                                              Content="{TemplateBinding SelectionBoxItem}"
                                              ContentTemplate="{TemplateBinding SelectionBoxItemTemplate}"
                                              Margin="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                                              HorizontalAlignment="{TemplateBinding HorizontalContentAlignment}"
                                              VerticalAlignment="{TemplateBinding VerticalContentAlignment}" />
                        </Border>
                    </DockPanel>

                    <!-- IsOpen must be TwoWay: ComboBox.PopupClosed does NOT write IsDropDownOpen
                         back (decompiled), so a light dismiss would leave the property stuck true
                         and the next click would do nothing. MinWidth off the templated parent's
                         Bounds.Width is what makes a 220 DIP combo drop a 220 DIP list. -->
                    <Popup x:Name="PART_Popup"
                           WindowManagerAddShadowHint="False"
                           IsOpen="{TemplateBinding IsDropDownOpen, Mode=TwoWay}"
                           MinWidth="{Binding Bounds.Width, RelativeSource={RelativeSource Mode=TemplatedParent}, Mode=OneWay}"
                           MaxHeight="{TemplateBinding MaxDropDownHeight}"
                           PlacementTarget="{TemplateBinding}"
                           IsLightDismissEnabled="True"
                           InheritsTransform="True">
                        <Border x:Name="PopupOuterBorder"
                                BorderBrush="{DynamicResource AeroListBoxOuterBorderBrush}"
                                BorderThickness="1"
                                CornerRadius="0">
                            <Border x:Name="PopupInnerBorder"
                                    Background="{DynamicResource AeroListBoxBackground}"
                                    BorderBrush="{DynamicResource AeroListBoxInnerBorderBrush}"
                                    BorderThickness="1"
                                    CornerRadius="0">
                                <ScrollViewer x:Name="PopupScrollViewer"
                                              HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="{TemplateBinding (ScrollViewer.HorizontalScrollBarVisibility)}"
                                              VerticalScrollBarVisibility="{TemplateBinding (ScrollViewer.VerticalScrollBarVisibility)}"
                                              IsDeferredScrollingEnabled="{TemplateBinding (ScrollViewer.IsDeferredScrollingEnabled)}">
                                    <ItemsPresenter x:Name="PART_ItemsPresenter"
                                                    ItemsPanel="{TemplateBinding ItemsPanel}" />
                                </ScrollViewer>
                            </Border>
                        </Border>
                    </Popup>
                </Panel>
            </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter>


        <!-- PointerOver State. The body follows Aero's text box, the button follows Aero's button;
             both change together because the whole control is one click target. -->
        <Style Selector="^:pointerover">
            <Style Selector="^ /template/ Border#BgElement">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroTextBoxHoverBackground}" />
                <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroTextBoxHoverBorderBrush}" />
            </Style>
            <Style Selector="^ /template/ Border#DropDownButtonOuter">
                <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonHoverOuterBorderBrush}" />
            </Style>
            <Style Selector="^ /template/ Border#DropDownButtonInner">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonHoverBackground}" />
                <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonHoverInnerBorderBrush}" />
            </Style>
        </Style>


        <!-- Pressed and DropDownOpen States. Both are the Aero button's pressed gradient, and both
             have to come after :pointerover, because a pressed control is also hovered and equal
             specificity is resolved by document order. -->
        <Style Selector="^:pressed, ^:dropdownopen">
            <Style Selector="^ /template/ Border#DropDownButtonOuter">
                <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonPressedOuterBorderBrush}" />
            </Style>
            <Style Selector="^ /template/ Border#DropDownButtonInner">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonPressedBackground}" />
                <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonPressedInnerBorderBrush}" />
            </Style>
        </Style>


        <!-- Focused State, and the ONLY focus signal this control has, because the FocusAdorner
             Setter above deliberately removes the other one. The field takes Aero's focus
             border-brush and background, which is what the vendored TextBox does for the same
             state and with the same two keys. §3.4 of the design wants keyboard focus drawn as the
             classic dotted rectangle rather than Windows 11's two rings; nothing in this file
             draws that rectangle, and no claim is made here that a repaint substitutes for it. It
             is the same position the vendored TextBox and NumericUpDown already ship in. -->
        <Style Selector="^:focus">
            <Style Selector="^ /template/ Border#BgElement">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroTextBoxFocusBackground}" />
                <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroTextBoxFocusBorderBrush}" />
            </Style>
        </Style>


        <!-- Disabled State, LAST, so it wins over every state above it. -->
        <Style Selector="^:disabled">
            <Style Selector="^ /template/ Border#BgElement">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroTextBoxDisabledBackground}" />
                <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroTextBoxDisabledBorderBrush}" />
            </Style>
            <Style Selector="^ /template/ Border#DropDownButtonOuter">
                <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonDisabledOuterBorderBrush}" />
            </Style>
            <Style Selector="^ /template/ Border#DropDownButtonInner">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonDisabledBackground}" />
                <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonDisabledInnerBorderBrush}" />
            </Style>
            <Style Selector="^ /template/ PathIcon#DropDownGlyph">
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AeroButtonDisabledForeground}" />
            </Style>
            <Style Selector="^ /template/ ContentPresenter#SelectionBoxPresenter">
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AeroDisabledForeground}" />
            </Style>
        </Style>
    </ControlTheme>
</ResourceDictionary>
